1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
 Thermoforming as a process is kind of like the polymer equivalent to forging of metals.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:11,520
 In thermoforming we heat a sheet of a thermoplastic polymer until it's nice and soft and pliable

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,760
 and then pull that sheet down over a positive of the shape that we'd like to make.

4
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,840
 There are a couple of different types of thermoforming but they're all kind of the same thing.

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,520
 Where the process is different slightly is in the application of pressure,

6
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:29,200
 whether there is any and what direction it acts into either push or suck the thermoplastic

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,640
 onto the part you'd like to make. In vacuum thermoforming, sometimes called vacuum forming

8
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:40,800
 and negative pressure is used to suck the soft pliable hot thermoplastic onto a negative

9
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,880
 a mould or a positive called a plug of the geometry that you'd like to make.

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,240
 In pressure thermoforming, sometimes just called pressure forming,

11
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:54,560
 a positive pressure is used to force the soft hot pliable thermoplastic over the shape you'd like to make.

12
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:59,040
 Thermoforming is quite an interesting process in that it's economical at both very small and

13
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:03,520
 very large scales. Hobbyists can purchase thermoforming machines that they can set up in their own

14
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:08,320
 homes for less than $1,000. And on the other end of the spectrum, thermoforming machines are often

15
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:13,280
 used to make very large thin parts that otherwise would be quite expensive to manufacture,

16
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:18,240
 say by injection molding or by blow molding. Components like the bodies of pools and spas, for example.

17
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:22,240
 I've left some excellent videos on thermoforming for you on the textbook page that's

18
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,280
 associated with this one. A video from Adam Savage talking about some hobbyist grade thermoforming

19
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:32,400
 machines and a video from how it's made talking about the manufacturing of pools and spas using

20
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:38,160
 thermoforming. I hope you enjoy them.
